<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Ogedeid Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1229 CE – 1310 CE (approx.) · capital Emil</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Central Asian realm of Ögedei's descendants, whose leader Kaidu waged a forty-year war against Kublai Khan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> gave his third son Ögedei pastures in the region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emil-river">Emil</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungaria</a>. Ögedei became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> in 1229, and his line ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>. After the throne passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toluids">Toluids</a> in 1251 they were purged, but Ögedei's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> rebuilt their power from the 1260s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> ruled from the Ili and Talas valleys, dominating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> through his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a> and challenging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan's</a> claim to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>. The khanate was a nomadic military power that drew revenue from the oasis towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim</a>, administered by officials such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masud-beg">Mas'ud Beg</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> died in 1301 after battle against Yuan forces. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapar">Chapar</a> made peace with the Yuan in 1304, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a> turned on him, and by 1310 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> had absorbed the Ogedeid lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ogedeid-khanate.jpg" alt="Ogedeid Khanate" width="268" height="400"><figcaption>Image: Bahatur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaidu%27s_tamga.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1229 CE – 1310 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Emil</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ögedei, Kaidu, Chapar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ulus of Ogedei, Kaidu's khanate, House of Ogedei</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1270&amp;focus=ogedeid-khanate" aria-label="Ogedeid Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7436 985 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ogedeid Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7559 780h0M6937 875h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7756 1241h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7756 1241h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=1270&amp;focus=ogedeid-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
